# Online Shopping Purchase Prediction

## 1. Context
The proposed client is a small online clothing retailer with basic website analytics and limited machine learning experience. The marketing team would use the system, supported by an analyst.

## 2. Problem and baseline
The store wants to understand which website visitors are likely to purchase. Currently, it reviews basic traffic and sales reports without predictive analytics. The current conversion rate would need to be measured before implementation.

## 3. Goal and success metrics
The business goal is to increase conversion rates through better targeting. A preliminary target is a 5% relative increase during a three-month pilot, subject to validation. The model would be evaluated using recall, with a proposed minimum threshold of 0.60 on unseen data. Response time and operating costs would also need to be assessed.

## 4. Solution
A basic classification model would predict whether a browsing session will result in a purchase. It would use browsing information such as the number of product pages viewed and time spent on the website. The marketing team could use predictions to support targeting decisions. If the model is unavailable, the store would continue using its existing reports.

## 5. Data
The project would use the public UCI Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention Dataset, containing 12,330 sessions. The target variable is Revenue, indicating whether a purchase occurred. The dataset is static, so a production system would require new data collection. Input availability during an ongoing session and privacy requirements would need to be checked.

Dataset: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/468/online+shoppers+purchasing+intention+dataset

## 6. Value, cost and risks
The potential benefit is improved targeting compared with basic reporting or fixed rules. A small student team could build an initial prototype using Python and GitHub over approximately two to three weeks. This is a preliminary effort estimate.

Risks include inaccurate predictions, outdated data and unavailable live-session features. These could be addressed through validation, monitoring and additional data collection. The approach could potentially be adapted to other online retailers.

## 7. Key takeaway
A basic MLOps pipeline could help an online retailer test purchase predictions consistently and monitor model performance over time.

## Open gaps
The actual conversion baseline, business impact, live data availability and production costs still need to be verified.
